$ unikraft mcp --help

The server speaks the Model Context Protocol over stdin and stdout, so an AI
assistant such as Claude Code, Claude Desktop or Cursor can manage Unikraft
Cloud through typed tools instead of shelling out to the CLI.

Every resource type gets list, create, edit and delete tools, named like
`instance_list` or `volume_create`; a list tool also fetches specific resources
when given keys. Instances additionally get `instance_control` for start, stop,
restart and suspend, and `instance_logs`. The tools use the current profile and
fan out across its metros exactly like the matching CLI commands do.

Pass `--resources` with a comma-separated list of resource types to serve
only their tools, such as `--resources instance,volume,metro`; the names
are the prefixes of the tool names. A shorter tool list costs an assistant
less context on every turn.

The documentation is served as resources: `unikraft://docs` is the table of
contents and `unikraft://docs/<path>` a page, such as
`unikraft://docs/cli/fields`.
Pages are fetched from the documentation site when read, so they cost
nothing until an assistant asks for one.

Stdout carries the protocol, so diagnostics go to stderr; raise them with
`--log-level debug`. A `--timeout` ends the whole session when it expires.

Register the server with a client as a stdio server, for example:

    {"mcpServers": {"unikraft": {"command": "unikraft", "args": ["mcp"]}}}

With `--listen host:port` the same tools are served over plain HTTP at `/mcp`,
with a liveness check at `/healthz`, still using this profile's credentials.
Only loopback addresses are accepted unless `--allow-remote` is given; when it
is, put an authenticating proxy or the platform's TLS termination in front,
since every client acts as you. A `--timeout` then stops accepting requests
and drains the calls in flight. Clients register the server by URL:

    {"mcpServers": {"unikraft": {"type": "http", "url":
"http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp"}}}

Usage:
  unikraft mcp [flags]

Examples:
  # Serve every tool over stdio
  unikraft mcp

  # Serve only the tools that cannot change anything
  unikraft mcp --read-only

  # Serve only the instance, volume and metro tools
  unikraft mcp --resources instance,volume,metro

  # Serve another profile with debug logs on stderr
  unikraft mcp --profile staging --log-level debug

  # Serve over HTTP on the loopback interface
  unikraft mcp --listen 127.0.0.1:8080

  # Serve every interface, for a hosted deployment behind the platform's TLS
  unikraft mcp --listen :8080 --allow-remote

Flags:
      --read-only
          Expose only the tools that cannot change anything: list, image_get and instance_logs.
      --resources=<type,...> ...
          Serve only the tools of these resource types, such as instance, volume or metro; the names are the prefixes of the tool names. Defaults to every type.
      --docs-url=<url> ($UNIKRAFT_DOCS_URL)
          Documentation site served as resources. Pass an empty value to serve none.
          [default: https://unikraft.com/docs/]
      --listen=<host:port>
          Serve the protocol over plain HTTP at this address, at /mcp, instead of stdio. Loopback addresses only unless --allow-remote is set.
      --allow-remote
          Let --listen bind a non-loopback address. Every client then acts with this profile's credentials, so only do this behind an authenticating proxy or on a private network.

Global flags:
  -h, --help
          Show context-sensitive help.
      --config=<file> ($UNIKRAFT_CONFIG)
          Path to the configuration file.
      --log-level=<level> ($UNIKRAFT_LOG_LEVEL)
          Set the logging level.
          [default: info, choices: trace, debug, info, warn, error, fatal]
      --log-type=<type> ($UNIKRAFT_LOG_TYPE)
          Set the log type.
          [default: text, choices: text, json]
      --profile=<name> ($UNIKRAFT_PROFILE)
          Set the current profile.
      --[no-]telemetry ($UNIKRAFT_TELEMETRY)
          Toggle usage analytics.
          [default: true]
      --timeout=<duration> ($UNIKRAFT_TIMEOUT)
          Set a deadline for the command (e.g. 30s, 5m, 1h).
